### \(\langle\)N\({}_{\rm bin}\rangle\,\)and \(\langle\)N\({}_{\rm part}\rangle\)

The hadronic cross section in heavy ion collisions is expected to scale with the mean number of participants (\(\langle\)N\({}_{\rm part}\rangle\,\)) for soft processes and the mean number of binary collisions (\(\langle\)N\({}_{\rm bin}\rangle\,\)) for hard processes. Therefore, hadron suppression at high p\({}_{\perp}\,\)may be observed by comparing the Au+Au yield per independent binary nucleon+nucleon (NN) collision with the yield of a reference system where jet quenching is not expected to occur. Two possible references are NN collisions or Au+Au collisions at large impact parameter. For an NN reference, hadron suppression is quantifiable via the nuclear modification factor:

\[R_{\rm AA}(p_{\perp})=\frac{{\rm dN}_{\rm AA}/{\rm dydp}_{\perp}}{(\langle{\rm N }_{\rm bin}\rangle/\sigma_{\rm NN}^{\rm inel}){\rm d}\sigma_{\rm NN}^{\rm inel}/{ \rm dydp}_{\perp}} \tag{5.10}\]

Absent nuclear medium effects, \(R_{\rm AA}=\)1 at high p\({}_{\perp}\,\). Jet quenching brings \(R_{\rm AA}\) below unity. Initial multiple scattering (Cronin) and radial flow increases \(R_{\rm AA}\,\), while nuclear shadowing decreases \(R_{\rm AA}\,\)2.

Footnote 2: See Motivation Chapter.

To determine \(\langle\)N\({}_{\rm bin}\rangle\,\)and \(\langle\)N\({}_{\rm part}\rangle\,\), d\(\sigma\)/dN\({}_{\rm bin}\) and d\(\sigma\)/dN\({}_{\rm part}\) were calculated using a Monte Carlo Glauber model with \(\sigma_{\rm NN}=41\pm 1\) mb. The number density ofnucleons follow the Woods-Saxon distribution

\[\rho(r)=\rho_{0}\frac{1}{1+\exp{(r-R)/a}}\,, \tag{5.11}\]

with radius \(R=6.5\pm 0.1\) fm and surface diffuseness \(a=0.535\pm 0.027\) fm. The parameters values were based on e+Au scattering data, and the radius was increased from \(R=6.38\) fm to \(R=6.5\) fm to account for the neutron skin [23]. The calculated \(\mathrm{d}\sigma/\mathrm{dN}_{\mathrm{bin}}\) (\(\mathrm{d}\sigma/\mathrm{dN}_{\mathrm{part}}\)) distribution was divided into percentile intervals, and was determined for each bin. The uncertainty on and were estimated by varying the Woods-Saxon parameters and \(\sigma_{\mathrm{NN}}\) according to their respective uncertainties, and by altering the the measured Au+Au cross section by \(\sim 5\%\). For the most peripheral bin, and were especially sensitive to the the measured Au+Au cross section, resulting in an uncertainty of \(\sim 30\%\)[84]. Table 5.1 lists and with their uncertainties for each centrality bin.

